    description: "Three Festuca glauca ‘Elijah Blue’ form compact cushions of intensely ice-blue, hair-fine leaves along the front-left edge. Narrow blue-green flower plumes rise in early summer and mature straw-gold, but the foliage is the main year-round feature. Rich or wet soil turns a dense blue tuft into a loose green one, while older clumps naturally open at the centre and are best renewed by division rather than hidden by shearing.",
    months: ["May", "Jun", "Jul"], size: "20–30cm", sizeDetail: "Each tuft spreads about 20–30cm", position: "Full sun", positionDetail: "Strong light maintains the bluest foliage", soil: "Poor to moderately fertile and drained", soilDetail: "Avoid wet clay pockets and rich feeding", hardiness: "H5", hardinessDetail: "Hardy with good drainage", feature: "Intense ice-blue evergreen tufts", featureDetail: "Fine plumes turn golden-brown in summer", identity: "Festuca glauca ‘Elijah Blue’", identityDetail: "Cultivar confirmed",
