    description: "The established Cherry Laurel forms a dense evergreen wall along the boundary, with large glossy leaves that screen the garden throughout winter. Upright clusters of small white flowers can appear in spring, followed by dark fruits where pollinated. It is naturally capable of reaching several metres in every direction, so selective cuts with secateurs preserve whole leaves and nesting cover better than repeatedly shredding the foliage with a hedge trimmer.",
    months: ["Apr", "May"], size: "4–8m if unrestricted", sizeDetail: "Can spread wider than 8m; maintained smaller by pruning", position: "Sun to shade", positionDetail: "Flowers more freely with reasonable light", soil: "Adaptable and well-drained", soilDetail: "Tolerates most moderately fertile soils", hardiness: "H5–H6", hardinessDetail: "Hardy throughout the UK", feature: "Dense glossy evergreen screen", featureDetail: "White flower spikes and dark ornamental fruit", identity: "Prunus laurocerasus", identityDetail: "Species secure; no smaller cultivar claimed",
